# Guest network config for the extension service VMs (stash, download-agent,
# pool-control). Shipped on the cidata seed alongside user-data / meta-data, and
# read by cloud-init's NoCloud datasource before networking is configured.
#
# The one thing this file exists to set is dhcp-identifier: mac.
#
# systemd-networkd defaults to identifying itself to the DHCP server with a DUID
# derived from /etc/machine-id. cloud-init SETS the machine-id and the hostname
# during first boot and restarts networking, so the guest re-requests under a
# client identity it did not have moments earlier. The server has no way to know
# it is the same machine: it treats each identity as a new client and leases a
# DIFFERENT address to each. A guest therefore moves address two or three times
# within the first seconds of its first boot.
#
# That churn is invisible from inside the guest and expensive outside it. macOS
# `bootpd` never prunes lease blocks, so every abandoned address stays on file
# under this guest's name; a host that resolves the address once then aims a
# port-forwarder, a readiness probe and a published URL at an address the guest
# has already left. Keying on the interface MAC -- which is fixed in the VM
# bundle and survives every machine-id and hostname change -- makes the guest one
# client for its whole life, so it takes one address and keeps it, on this boot
# and on every later one.
#
# Matched by name pattern rather than by MAC: this file is shared by every
# extension guest on every host type, and the interface is enp0s1 on UTM, eth0 on
# Hyper-V and enp1s0 on KVM. The two entries mirror cloud-init's own fallback
# network config, so a guest whose NIC matches neither is no worse off than it
# would have been with no file here at all.
version: 2
ethernets:
  yuruna-en:
    match:
      name: "en*"
    dhcp4: true
    dhcp6: true
    dhcp-identifier: mac
  yuruna-eth:
    match:
      name: "eth*"
    dhcp4: true
    dhcp6: true
    dhcp-identifier: mac
